# vtium's configuration, installed as /etc/vtium/vtium.conf.example. It is
# generated from crates/vtium-settings/src/keys.rs, and docs/config.md
# describes each key.
#
# One key a line: key=value, or a switch's key to turn it on and no-key to
# turn it off. Each key is shown at its default, commented out. The command
# line wins over this file.

# ══ Fonts and text ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The font vtium draws text with, its size, where box drawing and block
# characters come from, and how blinking text draws.

# ── Font ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## font-name: The font vtium draws text with.
##
## It is a frit's name, `NAME.vtfrit` in `/etc/vtium/fonts`, whose glyphs the
## helper generates at run time. A kbd font's name, or with `font-engine=psf`
## a PSF font's path, that no frit bears becomes a one-source frit.
##
## Unset, vconsole's font; with none, or a name that is neither, vtium draws
## its built-in fonts and logs the name and why.
#
## font-frits: The directory of the frits, the `NAME.vtfrit` files that
## `font-name` and a head's profile name. The default is the directory vtium
## was built with.
#
## font-engine: The font source to use. Unset, each the build has, in this
## order.

#font-name=
#font-frits=/etc/vtium/fonts
#font-engine=

# ── Font size and spacing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

## font-size: The font's size in points, 1/72 inch.
##
## The font draws at points × DPI ÷ 72 pixels at the displays' density
## (`display-dpi`), and the size wins over a frit's `cell=`. 0 picks a size
## from the display's size.
#
## font-hinting: How the font helper is to hint outline glyphs, over a frit's
## `hinting=`.
##
## `light` fits each glyph to the pixel grid up and down only; `full` fits a
## TrueType font both ways, and draws a CFF font as `light` does. Bitmap fonts
## take no hinting.
##
## vtium does not yet hand this key to the helper, so for now each frit's
## `hinting=` decides, `light` where it states none.
#
## font-line-spacing: Height added to each row, over a frit's `line-spacing=`.
##
## It is a whole percentage of the text's height, rounded to whole pixels and
## split evenly above and below the text, so the grid has fewer rows. It adds
## nothing to a frit's own `cell=`, but it applies when `font-size` sets the
## size over that `cell=`: the cell grows by the spacing, split evenly above
## and below each row. Unset, the frit's `line-spacing=` decides, 0 where it
## states none.
##
## vtium does not build line spacing yet: it accepts the key, and the key has
## no effect for now.

#font-size=0
#font-hinting=
#font-line-spacing=

# ── Box drawing, blocks and other sprites ───────────────────────────────────

## glyph-source: Where the six sprite ranges draw from.
##
## `font` draws the font's glyphs; `sprites`, vtium's; `auto`, the font's
## where it has one. Unset, a named font's glyphs for each range it covers
## whole and the sprites for the rest; the built-in unifont draws the sprites.
#
## glyph-source-box: `glyph-source` for box drawing, U+2500-U+257F, over it.
#
## glyph-source-block: `glyph-source` for block elements, U+2580-U+259F, over
## it.
#
## glyph-source-braille: `glyph-source` for Braille, U+2800-U+28FF, over it.
#
## glyph-source-sextants: `glyph-source` for sextants, U+1FB00-U+1FB3B, over
## it.
#
## glyph-source-octants: `glyph-source` for octants, U+1CD00-U+1CDE5, over it.
#
## glyph-source-legacy: `glyph-source` for the rest of Symbols for Legacy
## Computing's block shapes, over it.

#glyph-source=
#glyph-source-box=
#glyph-source-block=
#glyph-source-braille=
#glyph-source-sextants=
#glyph-source-octants=
#glyph-source-legacy=

# ── Missing characters ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## What a character the font lacks draws as: `box`, U+25A1; `hex`, its
## codepoint in hex digits.

#missing=box

# ── Blinking ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## blink: Blink text with SGR 5 and a blinking cursor, every 500 ms; off,
## neither blinks.
#
## blink-text: How text with SGR 5 or 6 draws in the blink's off phase.
##
## `full` hides it, `partial` draws it halfway to its background as SGR 2
## does, and `off` draws it steady. The cursor blinks as `blink` says either
## way.
#
## blink-decorations: The underline and strike of blinking text blink with it.
##
## They hide in the off phase with `blink-text=full`, and dim with `partial`.
## Off, they stay while the text blinks.

#blink
#blink-text=partial
#no-blink-decorations

# ── Font switching by programs ──────────────────────────────────────────────

## Let a program switch the font among the frits and the built-in fonts with
## xterm's OSC 50, and ask which is in use.
##
## A program switches with `OSC 50 ; NAME ST`, as `vtium-config set NAME`
## sends it, and asks with `OSC 50 ; ? ST`. A switch lasts until the session
## it came from ends, when vtium returns the console to the font and size
## `font-name` and `font-size` give.
##
## It is on by default, as xterm's `allowFontOps` is, since OSC 50 reaches
## only the frits root wrote and the built-in fonts, never a file. Off, vtium
## ignores OSC 50, the query included.

#allow-font-ops

# ══ Colors ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The sixteen colors programs draw with, the default foreground and
# background, and the colors of underlines and strikes.

# ── Palette ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## palette: The sixteen colors and the default foreground and background.
##
## Its value is a named palette, or `custom`, which takes the colors from the
## `palette-*` keys. Colors 16 to 255 are xterm's in every palette.
#
## palette-black: Color 0 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-red: Color 1 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-green: Color 2 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-yellow: Color 3 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-blue: Color 4 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-magenta: Color 5 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-cyan: Color 6 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-light-grey: Color 7 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-dark-grey: Color 8 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-light-red: Color 9 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-light-green: Color 10 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-light-yellow: Color 11 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-light-blue: Color 12 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-light-magenta: Color 13 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-light-cyan: Color 14 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-white: Color 15 with `palette=custom`.
## palette-foreground: The default foreground with `palette=custom`.
## palette-background: The default background with `palette=custom`.

#palette=legacy
#palette-black=0,0,0
#palette-red=205,0,0
#palette-green=0,205,0
#palette-yellow=205,205,0
#palette-blue=0,0,238
#palette-magenta=205,0,205
#palette-cyan=0,205,205
#palette-light-grey=229,229,229
#palette-dark-grey=127,127,127
#palette-light-red=255,0,0
#palette-light-green=0,255,0
#palette-light-yellow=255,255,0
#palette-light-blue=92,92,255
#palette-light-magenta=255,0,255
#palette-light-cyan=0,255,255
#palette-white=255,255,255
#palette-foreground=229,229,229
#palette-background=0,0,0

# ── Underline and strike colors ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## underline-color: The color every underline draws in, SGR 4 in each of its
## styles and SGR 21.
##
## `fg` draws it in the text's color, a palette index in that palette color,
## which follows a program's OSC 4, and `#rrggbb` in that color. A color a
## program sets with SGR 58 wins unless `force-decoration-colors` is on.
#
## strike-color: The color the strike, SGR 9, draws in, as `underline-color`
## takes it.
#
## force-decoration-colors: `underline-color` wins over the underline color a
## program sets with SGR 58.

#underline-color=fg
#strike-color=fg
#no-force-decoration-colors

# ══ Keyboard and pointer ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The keyboard's layout and repeat, the mouse and the pointer, and copying and
# pasting.

# ── Keyboard ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## xkb-model: The keyboard model, as xkeyboard-config names it.
## xkb-layout: The keyboard layout, such as `us` or `de`.
## xkb-variant: The layout's variant.
## xkb-options: xkb options, comma-separated.
## xkb-keymap: A keymap file, taken before the names above.
## xkb-compose-file: A compose table, taken before the locale's.
## xkb-repeat-delay: How long a held key waits before it repeats; 0 takes 250.
## xkb-repeat-rate: The time between a held key's repeats; 0 takes 50.

#xkb-model=
#xkb-layout=
#xkb-variant=
#xkb-options=
#xkb-keymap=
#xkb-compose-file=
#xkb-repeat-delay=250
#xkb-repeat-rate=50

# ── Pointer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## mouse: Take pointers: mice, touchpads and touchscreens.
#
## natural-scrolling: Invert the mouse wheel.
##
## The wheel scrolls the view and reports to a program the other way, and so
## do the cursor keys it sends on the alternate screen under mode 1007. Ctrl
## and the wheel still zoom in when the wheel turns up.
#
## soft-cursor: Draw the pointer in software on every display, never on a
## display's cursor plane.
#
## pointer-char: Draw the pointer as this character's glyph from the font.
##
## The glyph draws one cell wide, on the cell under the pointer, white with a
## dark outline as the arrow is. Unset, or a character the font lacks or whose
## glyph is blank, the pointer is the arrow.
#
## pointer-hide-timeout: Hide the pointer after this long with no pointer
## event, until it next moves; 0 never hides it.
#
## pointer-hide-while-typing: Hide the pointer at each key sent to the
## program, until it next moves. A key vtium takes, such as a grab, leaves it.

#mouse
#no-natural-scrolling
#no-soft-cursor
#pointer-char=
#pointer-hide-timeout=20
#no-pointer-hide-while-typing

# ── Clipboard and paste ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## clipboard-write: Take the selection a program sets with OSC 52; off, it is
## dropped.
##
## The console has no system clipboard: vtium holds the last selection taken,
## as it holds one the user copies, and the middle button and `grab-paste`
## paste it.
#
## grab-paste: Paste the selection buffer to the program, once a press, as the
## middle button does.
##
## A chord with Shift names the keysym Shift makes: `<Ctrl><Shift>V`, not
## `<Ctrl><Shift>v`. Empty, no key pastes.

#clipboard-write
#grab-paste=<Shift>Insert

# ══ Session and terminal ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The program each console runs, the terminal it sees, and how much of its
# output vtium keeps.

# ── The session's command ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

## login: The session's command.
##
## Unset, `/bin/login -p` with `reset-env` on and `/bin/login` with it off. A
## word that is `$NAME` becomes that variable of the session's environment,
## and `$$` starts a word with `$`.
#
## oneshot: Exit when the session's command exits, in place of starting it
## again.

#login=
#no-oneshot

# ── The session's terminal ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

## term: `TERM` in the session's environment.
##
## The default names vtium's own terminfo entry, and XTGETTCAP answers from
## that entry's capabilities; `kmscon` names the C reference's entry, and
## XTGETTCAP answers from the C reference's. Under any other name every name
## XTGETTCAP asks for is unknown.
#
## reset-env: Start the session with an empty environment and the variables
## vtium sets; off, with vtium's own environment as well.
#
## backspace-delete: The pty's erase character is DEL (0x7f); off, BS (0x08).

#term=xterm-vtium
#reset-env
#backspace-delete

# ── Scrollback ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## sb-size: Lines of scrollback, at most `sb-size-cap`.
#
## sb-size-cap: The most lines `sb-size` may ask for.
##
## Unset, it follows the machine's memory: 10,000 lines below 2 GiB, 30,000
## from 2 GiB and 100,000 from 4 GiB.
#
## sb-memory: The most bytes the scrollback holds: its oldest lines go past
## it, as past `sb-size`.
##
## It takes bytes, or KiB, MiB or GiB with `K`, `M` or `G` (`6M`), at most a
## quarter of the machine's memory, or at most the default when
## `/proc/meminfo` cannot be read.
##
## Left unset, the default follows each console's grid, worked out again when
## the grid changes: on a grid up to minspec's 170 by 48 it is what the 16 MiB
## minspec peak leaves after the console's two screens at their worst and the
## rest of vtium, so a smaller display keeps more, and a larger display keeps
## the 170 by 48 value shown here.
##
## The default does not grow with the display or the machine's memory; on a
## machine that can spare more, set `sb-memory` for a deeper scrollback, and
## it stays fixed.

#sb-size=1000
#sb-size-cap=
#sb-memory=5242880
# (in a build with images: sb-memory=4653056)

# ── Bell ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Rings the VT's bell when a program sends BEL.
##
## A program stops its own BELs ringing by resetting mode 1042, which starts
## set. Off, no BEL rings.

#no-bell

# ── Synchronized frames ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## The longest a synchronized frame (mode 2026) is held before it is shown as
## drawn; 0 offers no mode 2026.

#sync-timeout=200

# ══ Consoles and the VT ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# How many consoles the heads hold, and the VT vtium runs on.

# ── Consoles ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## head-consoles-max: The most consoles one head holds: the head file's
## `consoles` opens no more.
#
## consoles-max: The most consoles every head holds together.
##
## Each head keeps its first console; the others open, in the head file's
## order, while the total is below this.

#head-consoles-max=4
#consoles-max=8

# ── The VT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## vt: The VT to run on: a number N for `/dev/ttyN`, a name under `/dev`, or a
## path. Unset, the VT vtium was started on, or the next free one.
#
## switchvt: Switch to vtium's VT at start; off, wait until something else
## switches to it.

#vt=
#switchvt

# ══ On-screen display and notices ═══════════════════════════════════════════
#
# What vtium draws over the console to show which head and console are in
# front, and to tell of a problem, and the colors it draws them in.

# ── On-screen display ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## osd: What vtium's on-screen display draws at the top of each head after a
## change.
##
## With `minimal`, it draws a dot in the top-right cell, filled on the active
## head and hollow on the others. With `full`, it draws a line across the top,
## such as `● desk · console 2 of 3`. With `off`, it draws nothing.
##
## A notice of a problem shows whatever `osd` says.
#
## osd-timeout: How long the on-screen display stays after a change, counted
## from the first frame that draws it. `never` keeps it up from the start.
#
## focus-mark: The older name of `osd` and `osd-timeout`.
##
## vtium reads it only where `osd` or `osd-timeout` is not set: `auto` reads
## as `osd = minimal`, `always` as `osd = minimal` with `osd-timeout = never`,
## and `off` as `osd = off`. vtium logs a line naming the new keys when a file
## or the command line sets it.

#osd=minimal
#osd-timeout=3
#focus-mark=

# ── Notice colors ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## notice-foreground: The color of a notice's text. A notice tells of a
## problem, such as an invalid head file, and shows whatever `osd` says.
#
## notice-background: The color of a notice's box. While a notice shows, the
## on-screen display's full line shares its box and takes the notice's colors.
#
## notice-border: The color of the edge around a notice's box.

#notice-foreground=31,41,51
#notice-background=254,226,226
#notice-border=185,28,28

# ── On-screen display colors ────────────────────────────────────────────────

## osd-foreground: The color of the on-screen display's text: the words of
## `osd = full`'s line, and the dot of `osd = minimal`.
#
## osd-background: The color of the on-screen display's box behind
## `osd = full`'s line, and of the cell behind `osd = minimal`'s dot.
#
## osd-border: The color of the edge around `osd = full`'s box. The dot of
## `osd = minimal` has no edge.

#osd-foreground=229,231,235
#osd-background=31,41,51
#osd-border=97,110,124

# ── Other color schemes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Other schemes for the six colors above. To use one, remove the # at the
## start of its six lines. vtium draws whatever colors are set and checks no
## contrast between a text and its box.

## light: For a light terminal background: the on-screen display is a pale
## grey box with dark text and a grey edge, and a notice a dark red box with
## white text and a darker edge, so a problem differs from a status in
## brightness as well as in hue.

#notice-foreground=255,255,255
#notice-background=153,27,27
#notice-border=69,10,10
#osd-foreground=31,41,51
#osd-background=243,244,246
#osd-border=156,163,175

## dark: Both boxes are dark, for a dark terminal background: a notice is deep
## red with pale text and a light red edge, and the on-screen display near
## black with light grey text and a grey edge.

#notice-foreground=254,226,226
#notice-background=185,28,28
#notice-border=248,113,113
#osd-foreground=209,213,219
#osd-background=17,24,39
#osd-border=75,85,99

## color-blind-safe: For color blindness, a problem differs from a status in
## brightness and in an orange and blue pair, never in red against green: a
## notice is a bright orange box with near-black text, and the on-screen
## display a dark blue box with near-white text.

#notice-foreground=28,25,23
#notice-background=255,176,0
#notice-border=254,97,0
#osd-foreground=238,242,255
#osd-background=11,42,91
#osd-border=100,143,255

# ══ Displays ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The mode, depth and density of each display, how vtium waits for one at
# start, and how it draws to them.

# ── Display modes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## use-original-mode: Keep the mode each display is in; off, its preferred
## mode.
#
## mode: A display's mode.
##
## `WxH[@RATE]` sets it for every display, `[CONNECTOR]WxH` for one connector,
## and `GPU[CONNECTOR]WxH` for one connector on one card, the card named by
## its udev `ID_PATH`. A key per line; the most specific match wins. Unset,
## the panel's preferred mode. It turns `use-original-mode` off.

#no-use-original-mode
#mode=

# ── Display depth and density ───────────────────────────────────────────────

## display-depth: Each display's depth.
##
## `1` draws it in black and white, each cell lit or unlit by its colors,
## never by their brightness; `gray4` and `gray16` record a gray panel of 4 or
## 16 levels, drawn as color, which the panel converts; `color` draws its
## colors; `auto` takes the levels the display says it shows: an fbdev device
## at 1 bit, or a DRM panel whose driver vtium-platform lists.
#
## display-dpi: Each display's density, which converts `font-size` and a
## frit's `size=` from points to pixels, over the density a display measures.
##
## Unset, the density is measured from the physical size the display reports,
## else assumed at 96 with one logged warning per display.

#display-depth=auto
#display-dpi=

# ── Waiting for a display ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

## At start, how long to wait for a display, counted from when the VT is first
## shown.
##
## With no display by then, vtium exits with status 1, so the unit's
## `OnFailure=` can start a getty. Under a head file whose policy is closed
## vtium waits however long it takes, as under `forever` (ADR-1401 D8).
##
## 0 waits for no display to come: a display set up when the VT is first shown
## counts once its first frame is on screen. `forever` waits for the first
## display however long it takes, for a machine whose only display is a USB
## adapter (DisplayLink, `udl`) plugged in at any time.
##
## Once a display has come, vtium never exits for want of one.

#display-wait=10

# ── Display buffers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## How many buffers each DRM display keeps, the same for every display.
##
## `3` is triple buffering, so no frame waits for the last flip, at the cost
## of one more whole frame a display (8 MB at 1080p). `2` is double buffering.
## `1` is single buffering, which holds the least memory, but vtium then draws
## into the buffer being shown, so a frame can be seen half drawn.
##
## A framebuffer (fbdev) keeps one buffer whatever this key says. Unset, the
## build decides: 3 in a build with features on top of minspec, 2 in the
## minspec build, 1 only in a build smaller than minspec.

#display-buffers=

# ── Drawing through the GPU ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Draw through the GPU, in a build that has it.

#no-hwaccel

# ── Frame timer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Measure each display's frames once a second, and write the figures to the
## log, draw them on screen, or both.

#render-timer=none

# ══ Power and blanking ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# When the displays turn off, and the keys that reboot, power off or suspend
# the machine.

# ── Blanking the displays ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

## dpms-timeout: Turn the displays off after this long with no key press or
## pointer action (a move, a button or the wheel); 0 never does.
#
## program-blank: Let a program set the blank's timeout and wake the displays,
## as `setterm --blank` does under `TERM=linux`.
##
## A program sets the timeout with the Linux console's `CSI 9 ; n ]` (n
## minutes, at most 60; 0 never blanks) and wakes the displays with
## `CSI 13 ]`. RIS returns the timeout to `dpms-timeout`. Off, the sequences
## are ignored. A key press or pointer action wakes the displays either way.

#dpms-timeout=600
#program-blank

# ── Power keys ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## grab-reboot: Reboot the machine.
##
## With `/proc/sys/kernel/ctrl-alt-del` at 0, vtium asks init, as for
## Ctrl+Alt+Del, so the services stop first; otherwise the kernel restarts at
## once. Unset, no key reboots.
#
## grab-power-off: Power the machine off.
##
## vtium asks init with `SIGRTMIN+4`, which systemd takes as
## `poweroff.target`, so the services stop and the filesystems are unmounted
## first. Unset, no key powers off.
#
## grab-suspend: Suspend the machine to RAM.
##
## vtium asks logind with its `Suspend` method, so its inhibitors hold the
## sleep; where logind is not running, or vtium is built without `logind`,
## vtium writes `mem` to `/sys/power/state`. Unset, no key suspends.

#grab-reboot=
#grab-power-off=
#grab-suspend=

# ══ Pictures ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Sixel and kitty graphics: how much memory pictures take, which protocols
# vtium offers, and the helper that reads and decodes them.

# ── Picture memory and protocols ────────────────────────────────────────────

## image-storage-limit: The most bytes each console's pictures hold, its main
## and alternate screens together.
##
## It takes bytes, or KiB, MiB or GiB with `K`, `M` or `G` (`64M`), at most 1
## TiB. `auto` gives each console four frames of its head's displays, 16 MiB
## to 192 MiB, and works it out again when a display joins or leaves the head
## or changes size. `0` turns sixel and kitty graphics off.
##
## A build without images reads it and draws no picture.
#
## sixel: Sixel graphics.
##
## Off, vtium neither offers nor decodes sixel: DA1 reports no attribute 4 and
## a sixel string is ignored. vtium offers sixel only while
## `image-storage-limit` is above 0 and every display of the console's head
## draws pictures. A build without images never offers it.
#
## kitty-graphics: The kitty graphics protocol.
##
## Off, vtium neither offers nor decodes it: a graphics command is ignored and
## a query gets no reply. vtium offers it only while `image-storage-limit` is
## above 0 and every display of the console's head draws pictures. A build
## without images never offers it.
#
## max-graphic-size: The largest picture a program may send, in pixels, `WxH`,
## for sixel and kitty graphics alike; a larger one is refused.
##
## A build without images reads it and draws no picture.

#image-storage-limit=auto
#sixel
#kitty-graphics
#max-graphic-size=10000x10000

# ── Sixel color registers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

## num-color-registers: How many color registers a sixel picture has, and the
## most XTSMGRAPHICS lets a program set.
##
## A build without images reads it and draws no picture.
#
## private-color-registers: Mode 1070's state at a reset.
##
## On, each sixel picture starts from the default colors; off, the pictures
## share their color registers. A build without images reads it and draws no
## picture.

#num-color-registers=1024
#private-color-registers

# ── Image helper ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## The image helper's program, which vtium starts as the session's user to
## read the files and shared memory a kitty graphics command names and to
## decode PNG and zlib-compressed pictures, since vtium itself, running as
## root, does neither.
##
## vtium opens it once at start and starts every helper from that file, so a
## helper installed later reaches the consoles when vtium starts again.
##
## vtium refuses a path that is not absolute, a file that is not a regular
## file, a setuid or setgid file, a file owned by an account other than root
## or the account vtium runs as, and a file its group or others may write; it
## logs why, and every console then answers those pictures itself, a file, a
## temporary file or shared memory with `EBADF` and PNG or zlib with `EINVAL`.
## It starts the helper through `/proc/self/fd`, so `/proc` must be mounted.
##
## The default is the path vtium was built with. A build without images reads
## it and starts no helper.

#image-helper=/usr/bin/vtium-image

# ══ Running vtium ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Where vtium finds its configuration, its log, the glyph helper that draws a
# font's glyphs, and the glyph cache.

# ── Debug log ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## Log debug messages as well, to find what went wrong. Off, vtium logs its
## information messages, warnings and errors.

#no-debug

# ── Glyph helper ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## The glyph helper's program, which vtium starts to draw a frit's glyphs, as
## `nobody` when vtium runs as root.
##
## vtium opens it once at start and starts every helper from that file, so a
## helper installed later reaches a console when the console starts again.
##
## vtium refuses a path that is not absolute, a file that is not a regular
## file, a setuid or setgid file, a file owned by an account other than root
## or the account vtium runs as, and a file its group or others may write; it
## logs why and draws its built-in font. It starts the helper through
## `/proc/self/fd`, so `/proc` must be mounted.
##
## The default is the path vtium was built with.

#font-helper=/usr/bin/vtium-config

# ── Glyph cache ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## The glyph cache's budgets. Unset, `minspec`, or `performance` in a build
## with vtium-render's `performance-cache` feature.

#glyph-cache=
